        <span class="eyebrow">Guide</span>
        <h1>How to become a cleaner in New Zealand.</h1>
        <p class="lead">You usually don&rsquo;t need a qualification or years of experience to start cleaning work in New Zealand, and with us you don&rsquo;t need a CV either. You do need work rights, reliability, and a realistic picture of your own availability. Here&rsquo;s the whole path, step by step.</p>
        <p class="art-meta">Published 22 August 2026 &middot; by the Cleaning Jobs team</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="art-body">
      <div class="wrap-narrow">
        <h2>1. Check you can legally work in New Zealand</h2>
        <p>This is the one hard requirement. You need to be an NZ citizen or resident, or hold a visa with work rights. Every legitimate operator will ask, and with us it&rsquo;s a question on the application form, verified during onboarding for suitable applicants. If you&rsquo;re unsure about your own situation, <a href="https://www.immigration.govt.nz/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Immigration New Zealand</a> is the authority to check with, not a cleaning company.</p>

        <h2>2. You usually don&rsquo;t need qualifications to start</h2>
        <p>Cleaning is one of the trades you learn by doing. Tahat&#363;, the government&rsquo;s career platform, puts it plainly for commercial cleaners: you <a href="https://tahatu.govt.nz/work/explore-career-ideas/occupation/T00595-commercial-cleaner" target="_blank" rel="noopener">usually learn on the job, and usually don&rsquo;t need specific NCEA levels</a>. Depending on the role, you may need a driver licence, or to pass a police check. Good operators then train you to their own standard; with Spruce, that means training or assessment built around the same checklists used on every clean.</p>

        <h2>3. Decide how you want to work</h2>
        <p>Cleaning work in New Zealand comes in several shapes: employed roles, self-employed contracting, and franchise arrangements where you buy into a business. They differ in cost, in flexibility, and in how tax works. Two useful official starting points: <a href="https://www.employment.govt.nz/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Employment New Zealand</a> for employee rights and minimum entitlements, and <a href="https://www.ird.govt.nz/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Inland Revenue</a> for the difference between employees and self-employed contractors. You don&rsquo;t need to have this all figured out before applying anywhere; with us, the working arrangement is confirmed during onboarding.</p>

        <h2>4. Sort the practical basics</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Transport.</strong> You need your own reliable vehicle. Public transport alone isn&rsquo;t enough to reach the properties cleaners work at, so this is a requirement, not a preference.</li>
          <li><strong>Equipment.</strong> Depends on the work. Residential cleans often use basic gear of your own; commercial sites frequently supply their own.</li>
          <li><strong>Availability.</strong> Be honest about the hours you can actually offer. Mornings, evenings, weekends, or flexible: cleaning has room for all of them, and pretending otherwise helps nobody.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>5. Apply, once</h2>
        <p>The traditional route is chasing listings one at a time: CV, cover letter, silence, repeat. Our model is one application. You fill in <a href="/apply/">a single form</a> with your location, experience (&ldquo;None yet&rdquo; is accepted), transport, availability, and the work types that interest you. Every application is reviewed individually, suitable applicants are verified and trained to the Spruce standard, and certified cleaners become eligible for work allocation. The full pathway is on <a href="/how-it-works/">the how it works page</a>. One honest caveat: work is not guaranteed.</p>

        <h2>What about pay?</h2>
        <p>Pay depends on the role, the arrangement, and the hours, so treat any single number with suspicion. For independent benchmarks, <a href="https://tahatu.govt.nz/work/explore-career-ideas/occupation/T00595-commercial-cleaner" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tahat&#363; publishes indicative pay for cleaning occupations</a>, and the minimum wage is set by the government and published by <a href="https://www.employment.govt.nz/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Employment New Zealand</a>.</p>

        <p class="art-note">This guide links to official sources (Immigration New Zealand, Employment New Zealand, Inland Revenue, Tahat&#363;) for facts that sit outside our own process. It describes the Cleaning Jobs pathway as it works today and is not legal, tax, or immigration advice.</p>
      </div>
